  "body": "## Problem\r\n\r\nBoth firmware install paths — SD-card flash (`FirmwareFlasher`) and WiFi/GitHub OTA (`OtaUpdater`) — did **no** MCU-family compatibility check and deliberately bypass ESP-IDF's `esp_image_verify` (it rejects our patched image on X4 silicon). An image built for the wrong chip (ESP32-S3 \"Sticky\" firmware on an ESP32-C3 X3/X4, or vice versa) could be flashed and brick the device.\r\n\r\n## Mechanism\r\n\r\nEvery ESP32 app image begins with a 24-byte `esp_image_header_t`; byte offset 12 holds `chip_id` (uint16 LE). Instead of enumerating known chip IDs, this reads the **running** partition's own `chip_id` — it booted successfully, so it is authoritative for the current CPU — and requires the candidate image to match. Self-describing and future-proof: new chip variants (C6, P4, …) work with zero code changes. If the running `chip_id` is unreadable (`0xFFFF`), the check is skipped so a read failure never blocks a legitimate update.\r\n\r\n## Changes\r\n\r\n- **`FirmwareFlasher`**: new `runningPartitionChipId()` (reads the running slot once, cached in a function-local static) and a `BAD_CHIP` result. `validateImageFile()` rejects a mismatched image immediately after the magic-byte check — covering the SD flow and its TOCTOU re-validation at flash time.\r\n- **`OtaUpdater`**: accumulates the first 14 header bytes of the streamed download and checks `chip_id` **before** `esp_ota_write` commits it; aborts the transfer + `esp_ota_abort` on mismatch and returns a new `WRONG_DEVICE_ERROR`.\r\n- **Activities**: `SdFirmwareUpdateActivity` and `OtaUpdateActivity` surface `tr(STR_FIRMWARE_WRONG_DEVICE)` (\"Firmware is for a different device\").\r\n",
